DTSTAMP:20260727T105033Z
DESCRIPTION:Join us for a Deaf-led visual sharing circle with Charu Narang\, founder of DeafWomenToo (DWT). Inspired by warrior garments\, this workshop invites Deaf participants to explore shared memories and lived experiences through guided visual activities\, including body mapping and creative expression. Together\, we will create space for conversations around trauma\, anger\, healing\, and self-expression in a supportive environment.\n\nParticipants are welcome to engage at their own comfort level—whether through making\, sharing\, reflecting\, or simply being present.\n\nPlease note: This workshop supports personal reflection and self-expression. It is not a therapy session. The session will be conducted in Indian Sign Language (ISL)\, with interpretation into spoken English.\n\nThis session is part of the closing weekend of our exhibition warrior garments and Disability Pride programming 2026.
DTEND:20260801T113000Z
DTSTART:20260801T093000Z
SUMMARY:A Visual Sharing Circle on Trauma\, Anger\, Care and Boundaries
UID:1360@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/a-visual-sharing-circle-on-trauma-anger-care-and-boundaries/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/4faa8439-3889-49cc-bcfc-a3fe13310e80-e1785054302655.webp
CATEGORIES: Featuring,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260727T052132Z
DESCRIPTION:Celebrating National Handloom Day\, we have Vaishnavi Kambadur and Mamatha G P K introducing us to the history and mechanics of handlooms. \n\nThe session explores the cultural and social story of the cloth\, its smell (natural dyes like Kalamkari and indigo)\, motif (Ikat\, Bandhani\, Ajrakh)\, historic revivals (Chintz\, Baluchari)\, the weaving communities and castes behind specific textiles (Banarasi\, Pochampally\, Jamdani)\, and regional origins (Patola\, Chanderi\, Kanjeevaram). Following this\, it covers the technical side\, its loom types (pit\, frame\, shuttle\, jacquard)\, fabric count (Muslin vs. khadi)\, and hand vs. power loom distinctions. Having covered the history\, the session dives into handlooms in contemporary culture\, their presence in film costuming and social media trends like converting sarees into western silhouettes. The session ends with an interactive session that helps you identify different textiles from the MAP’s collections\, with a hands-on textile interaction session where you can touch and examine the swatches used throughout. \n\n&nbsp\;
DTEND:20260807T130000Z
DTSTART:20260807T113000Z
SUMMARY:Between the Looms - The Hands that Time Unearthed
UID:1358@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/between-the-looms/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://map-india.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/1440-X-810-Widget-04.jpg
CATEGORIES:Talks,MAP,CBD
DTSTAMP:20260727T112359Z
DESCRIPTION:Join us for a Human Library led by Rashmi Malhotra\, founder of India Deaf Queer\, as they share their lived experiences and invite conversations around access\, identity\, and everyday life. Through this session\, we will also reflect on the importance of making cultural spaces more inclusive and accessible.\n\nInspired by MAP's exhibition warrior garments by afra eisma\, the session invites participants to reflect on how emotions\, identity\, and intersectional experiences are carried and expressed. Through Indian Sign Language (ISL)\, Rashmi creates a space for dialogue\, reflection\, and exchange with Deaf and queer audiences. You are welcome to join\, listen\, ask questions\, or simply be present.\n\nPlease note: This session will be conducted in Indian Sign Language (ISL) with interpretation into spoken English.\n\nThis session is happening as part of the closing weekend of our exhibition warrior garments and Disability Pride programming 2026
DTEND:20260801T093000Z
DTSTART:20260801T083000Z
SUMMARY:Embodied Language: A Deaf-Led Human Library on warrior garments
UID:1359@map-india.org
URL:https://map-india.org/map-events/embodied-language-a-deaf-led-human-library-on-warrior-garments/
